        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A crawl space dehumidifier has a harder job than almost any other dehumidifier in the house. It runs in a dark, cramped, often cold space that nobody visits, it may run thousands of hours a year, and when it fails, nobody notices until the floor above smells musty or the joists start growing mold. So we did not start from the list of best-selling dehumidifiers. We started from the list of units that were designed for crawl spaces in the first place.
          </p>

          <p>
            That filter removed almost every plastic portable dehumidifier on the market. What remained were compact, low-profile units from AlorAir, Aprilaire and Santa Fe, plus one LGR restoration unit that crawl space owners increasingly buy for recovery work. We then compared them on four things that decide whether a crawl space stays dry: rated capacity at realistic temperatures, minimum operating temperature, drainage options and physical size.
          </p>

          <p>
            Capacity at realistic temperatures was the most important of the four. Every crawl space unit is sold on its AHAM pint rating at 80°F and 60% relative humidity, and almost no crawl space is 80°F. We gave credit to manufacturers that publish a second figure at cooler conditions, such as the Aprilaire E070 rating at 73°F and the AlorAir HD90 rating at 60°F, because those numbers let you size honestly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size is the spec people forget until the unit arrives. Many crawl spaces have 18 to 24 inches of clearance under the beams and an access hatch not much bigger than the machine, so a 12-inch-tall unit and an 18-inch-tall unit are not interchangeable. We list the height of every pick where the manufacturer publishes it, and you should measure the hatch, the lowest beam and the path to the install spot before you order.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also looked at warranty length, filter quality, whether the unit can be ducted and what owners report after a year or two of continuous running. If you are drying a basement rather than a crawl space, our guide to the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best dehumidifiers for basements</a> covers the portable and whole-house units that make more sense there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="encapsulate-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Encapsulate First, Then Dehumidify
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most expensive mistake in this category is buying a crawl space dehumidifier for a vented crawl space. A dehumidifier dries the air inside a volume. If that volume is open to the outside through foundation vents and sitting on bare soil, it is not drying a crawl space. It is trying to dry the neighborhood, and it will run nonstop, rack up a large electric bill and still lose on a humid July day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bare soil is the other half of the problem. The ground under a house releases water vapor continuously, and a crawl space with an exposed dirt floor can take on many gallons of moisture a day from evaporation alone. Before any machine goes in, the floor should be covered with a continuous vapor barrier, seams overlapped and taped, run up the foundation walls and sealed at the top. Our comparison of <a href="/vapor-barrier-vs-waterproofing-membrane/">vapor barriers versus waterproofing membranes</a> explains which material does what and why a thin 6-mil sheet laid loose on the dirt is not encapsulation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Full encapsulation usually means four things: a reinforced liner of 10 to 20 mil on the floor and up the walls, sealed foundation vents, a sealed or insulated rim joist and a sealed access door. Once those are done, the crawl space becomes a small conditioned volume, and a dehumidifier sized for it can hold the humidity at 50% to 55% with modest run time. That is the environment every unit in this guide is designed for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recent editions of the International Residential Code reflect the same logic. The code allows an unvented crawl space when the ground is covered with a sealed vapor retarder and the space is either supplied with conditioned air or dehumidified, and the dehumidification option is sized around 70 pints per day per 1,000 square feet of floor area. Your local code may differ, so check before you seal vents, but the principle is universal: seal first, then dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            If water is actually entering the crawl space as liquid, from a high water table, poor grading or a plumbing leak, deal with that before encapsulation too. A dehumidifier removes vapor. It is not a sump pump, and no amount of pint capacity will keep up with standing water.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A dehumidifier cannot out-run an open crawl space.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Seal the vents, cover the soil with a taped liner that runs up the walls, and close the rim joist before you buy. A 55-pint unit in a sealed crawl space will outperform a 90-pint unit in a vented one, and it will cost far less to run.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

