        <div id="low-temp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Low-Temperature Performance and LGR Design
          </h2>

          <p>
            Crawl spaces are cool. Even in summer, a sealed crawl space in most of the country sits somewhere in the 60s, and in winter it can drop into the 40s or lower. That matters because a refrigerant dehumidifier works by chilling air below its dew point so water condenses on the coil. The cooler and drier the incoming air, the lower the coil has to run, and below the mid-60s an ordinary portable dehumidifier starts to ice its coil and spend more time defrosting than drying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Purpose-built crawl space units handle this in two ways. First, they are engineered to keep working at low air temperatures, and their spec sheets say so: AlorAir rates its Sentinel units down to 33.8°F, Aprilaire rates the E070 down to about 40°F, and Santa Fe rates the Compact70 down to 49°F. Second, they defrost intelligently, cycling the compressor off while the fan clears the coil and then resuming, rather than simply shutting down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Low grain refrigerant, or LGR, is a related idea borrowed from the restoration industry. A grain is a unit of moisture, and LGR units are designed to keep extracting water as the air gets drier, where a conventional dehumidifier loses efficiency quickly. That is why restoration companies use them to dry flooded buildings, and why a unit like the AlorAir Storm LGR Extreme makes sense after water intrusion. For a sealed crawl space in steady state, a well-designed compact unit like the HD55 or Compact70 is usually enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is to read the minimum operating temperature before you buy, and to match it to your climate. In the Deep South, a 49°F floor may never matter. In Ohio or Pennsylvania, a crawl space can sit in the low 40s for weeks, and a unit rated to 33.8°F or 40°F will keep holding the humidity while a 49°F unit sits idle. Moisture load is lower in winter, but it is not zero, especially in the shoulder seasons.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">MINIMUM OPERATING TEMPERATURE</p>
