        <div id="pint-ratings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Pint Ratings: AHAM at 80°F vs the 2019 Standard
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dehumidifier pint ratings are genuinely confusing, and crawl space shoppers get caught by it more than anyone. There are three different ratings in circulation, and they describe the same machine under different conditions, so a 70-pint crawl space unit and a 50-pint basement portable may be closer in real output than the numbers suggest.
          </p>

          <p>
            The AHAM rating is the older industry standard: pints removed per day at 80°F and 60% relative humidity. Almost every crawl space unit is still marketed on this figure, including the 55-pint HD55, the 70-pint E070 and the 90-pint HD90. It is a useful comparison number within the category, but 80°F and 60% is warmer and more humid than a sealed crawl space almost ever gets.
          </p>

          <p>
            In 2019, the Department of Energy changed the test for portable dehumidifiers to 65°F and 60%, which is closer to real basement conditions. Cooler air holds much less water, so the same machine removes fewer pints under the new test. A portable once sold as 70 pints is now sold as 50 pints, and nothing about the machine changed. Whole-home units are tested under a separate DOE condition of 73°F and 60%, which is why the Aprilaire E070 lists both 70 pints at 80°F and 53 pints at 73°F.
          </p>

          <p>
            Saturation ratings are the third number, usually measured at 90°F and 90% relative humidity. They produce dramatic figures such as 120 pints for the HD55 or 198 pints for the HD90, and they describe conditions you will see only in a flooded space. Treat them as a measure of how quickly a unit can recover a very wet space, not as its everyday output.
          </p>

          <p>
            For sizing, use the AHAM number to compare crawl space units with each other, and use the lower published figures as your sanity check. Our guide on <a href="/what-size-dehumidifier-for-basement/">what size dehumidifier you need for a basement</a> walks through the same conversion if you are comparing a crawl space unit with a portable.
          </p>

