        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sizing a Crawl Space Dehumidifier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Crawl space sizing is simpler than basement sizing because the space is small, sealed and has a fairly predictable moisture load once it is encapsulated. The code-referenced starting point is about 70 pints per day, at AHAM conditions, for every 1,000 square feet of crawl space floor. That is a conservative figure with headroom built in, and it lines up with how manufacturers size their units.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most homes, that means a unit in the 55 to 70 pint class. A sealed 800 to 1,300 square foot crawl space in a moderate climate is well served by the AlorAir HD55. A 1,300 to 2,200 square foot crawl space, or a smaller one in a very humid climate, is where the Aprilaire E070 and Santa Fe Compact70 fit. Above about 2,000 square feet, or where encapsulation is incomplete, the HD90 or Advance90 make sense. Small, fully sealed spaces under about 1,000 square feet can get away with the 35-pint HD35P.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adjust for a few conditions. A crawl space in a humid coastal or Gulf climate should be sized up one step. So should one with a high water table, a history of standing water or a liner that is not yet sealed to the walls. A crawl space that shares air with a basement, or that is served by the HVAC system, should be sized for the combined volume rather than just the crawl space floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not oversize by too much, though. A unit far larger than the load pulls the humidity down quickly, shuts off, and cycles on and off all day. That short-cycling is harder on the compressor and does a worse job of drying the wood framing, which responds to long, steady periods of low humidity rather than brief dips.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are weighing a crawl space unit against a larger system that serves the whole house, our comparison of <a href="/whole-house-vs-portable-dehumidifier/">whole-house versus portable dehumidifiers</a> covers when a ducted whole-home unit is the better investment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Condensate Drain vs Built-In Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            A crawl space dehumidifier running in humid weather can produce several gallons of water a day, and none of the units in this guide has a bucket. The water has to go somewhere automatically, and deciding where is the part of the install that fails most often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gravity drainage is the simplest and most reliable option. If the crawl space has a floor drain, a sump pit or a grade that falls away from the house, run the drain tube downhill with a steady slope, no sags and no uphill sections, and terminate it where the water cannot run back toward the foundation. Most units ship with 10 to 20 feet of tube. A sump pit is a common and sensible destination, because the sump pump already handles the water.
          </p>

          <p>
            When there is no downhill route, you need a pump. Some crawl space units have one built in, such as the AlorAir HD35P, HD55P and the Storm LGR Extreme, and those are the tidiest installs because the pump is sized and wired for the unit. Others, including the Aprilaire E070 and Santa Fe Compact70, need a separate condensate pump, typically a small reservoir pump that lifts water up and out through the rim joist.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever route you take, protect the discharge. Run the line outside at least a few feet from the foundation or into a drain, keep the outdoor end from freezing in winter and check it every season for kinks and slime buildup. A blocked drain line leaves water in the unit, and a unit that stops draining will either shut off or spill onto the liner. Our guide to the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-with-pump/">best dehumidifiers with a built-in pump</a> covers pump lift, hose runs and the ways built-in pumps fail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ducting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Ducting, Placement and Airflow
          </h2>

          <p>
            A crawl space is usually long, narrow and obstructed by piers, ductwork and plumbing. A dehumidifier dries the air it pulls in, and in a cramped, cluttered space that air does not always mix well. Where you put the unit, and whether you duct it, decides whether the whole crawl space dries or just the corner nearest the hatch.
          </p>

          <p>
            For an average rectangular crawl space, place the unit near the center, raised off the liner on blocks or a stand, with at least a foot of clear space around the intake and the exhaust. That keeps the filter from pulling in dust and lets the dry air spread in both directions. Avoid tucking it against a wall or behind a pier, where it will mostly re-dry its own exhaust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ducting solves the problem in larger or oddly shaped spaces. Many crawl space units, including the AlorAir Sentinel models with their 6-inch duct collar and the Santa Fe Advance90 with its dual outlet design, let you run flexible duct to pull return air from the dampest end of the space or push supply air to a distant corner. A single duct run of 10 to 20 feet is usually enough, and it is well within what these blowers can handle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ducting also matters when you want the crawl space dehumidifier to help the house above. Some homeowners run a supply duct from the unit into the return side of the HVAC system or into a basement. That can work, but it is a job to plan carefully, because you are now moving air between spaces, and codes around combustion appliances and return air still apply. When in doubt, have an HVAC contractor lay it out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the unit accessible. You will need to reach the filter every few months, and a unit wedged behind ductwork at the far end of a 40-foot crawl space will not get its filter cleaned.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="portable-fail" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Why Portable Dehumidifiers Fail in Crawl Spaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            It is tempting to push a 50-pint portable into a crawl space and call it done. The portables cost a fraction as much, they are sold everywhere and many advertise a low-temperature setting. In our experience, and in the experience of most crawl space contractors, they rarely last, and there are specific reasons why.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first is temperature. Most portables are rated to operate down to the low 40s, but their capacity collapses well before that. At the 55°F to 65°F common in a crawl space, a portable spends much of its time defrosting, and its real output is a fraction of the number on the box. Crawl space units are engineered for those temperatures and still deliver useful capacity there.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is drainage and duty cycle. A portable is designed to run for a season in a room someone visits. In a crawl space it has to run year after year with nobody watching, drain continuously through a hose or pump and restart on its own after every power cut. Buckets are useless, gravity hose ports on portables are small and easy to kink, and the built-in pumps on portables are the part that fails first when they run constantly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is construction and warranty. Crawl space units use metal housings, corrosion-resistant coils and serviceable filters, and they carry warranties of five or six years. Portables use thin plastic housings and typically carry a year or two of coverage. In a damp space, that difference in build quality shows up as corroded coils and dead fans. Portables also cannot be ducted, so they only dry the air around them.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this means portables are bad machines. In a finished or semi-finished basement, where temperatures sit in the 60s and 70s and someone checks on the unit, a good portable is the right tool. Our guide to the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best basement dehumidifiers</a> covers those. A crawl space is simply a different environment, and it rewards a different kind of machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="monitoring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Setpoints, Monitoring and Maintenance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once the unit is in, set it and verify it. Most crawl space contractors aim for 50% to 55% relative humidity. That is comfortably below the roughly 60% level where mold and dust mites get a foothold, and it keeps wood framing well below the moisture content where rot and fungal growth start. Setting it much lower than 45% wastes energy for little benefit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not rely on the dehumidifier's own display to tell you the space is dry. Its sensor reads the air entering the unit, which can differ noticeably from the far end of a long crawl space. Put an independent digital hygrometer at the opposite end, ideally a wireless one you can read from inside the house. Our guide to the <a href="/best-hygrometer/">best hygrometers</a> covers accurate, inexpensive options that log readings over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the numbers for the first few weeks. If the far end reads within a few points of the setpoint, the unit and placement are right. If it reads much higher, either the unit is too small, the air is not mixing or there is a moisture source you have not dealt with, such as a gap in the liner, an open vent or a plumbing leak.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is simple but easy to forget. Clean or replace the filter every two to three months, more often in a dusty crawl space. Check the drain line and discharge point at least twice a year and after any hard freeze. Every year or two, inspect the coil for dust buildup and clean it gently. A clogged filter cuts airflow, raises coil temperatures and quietly reduces capacity, which is why many owners notice the crawl space creeping up in humidity in the unit's third or fourth year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep an eye on the house above. A musty smell returning, cupping hardwood floors or condensation on ductwork in the crawl space are all signs the humidity has drifted up, and they usually show up before visible mold does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a dehumidifier for a vented crawl space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the foundation vents are open and the soil is bare, the unit is trying to dry outdoor air and ground moisture that never stop coming. It will run constantly, cost a lot to operate and still lose in humid weather. Seal the vents, install a taped liner and close the rim joist before you buy any machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing on the saturation rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A saturation figure measured at 90°F and 90% humidity describes a flooded room, not a sealed crawl space. Compare units on their AHAM rating at 80°F and 60%, then use any cooler-condition figure the maker publishes as a reality check. Sizing on saturation numbers leaves most crawl spaces with too small a machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not measuring the access hatch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Crawl space units range from about 12 inches tall to nearly 20. Many access openings and beam clearances are smaller than people assume, and a unit that will not fit through the hatch is an expensive return. Measure the hatch, the lowest beam and the path to the install spot before ordering, and add an inch for handling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the drain line to chance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A drain tube with a sag, an uphill section or a discharge that freezes in winter will back water into the unit and shut it down. Plan the route before install day, slope gravity lines steadily, use a pump when there is no downhill path, and inspect the discharge end every season and after hard freezes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the unit's built-in humidity display</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The onboard sensor reads the air entering the machine, which is often drier than the far corner of a long crawl space. Place an independent hygrometer at the opposite end and check it for the first few weeks. If it reads much higher than the setpoint, the unit is undersized, badly placed or fighting a leak.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier do I need for a crawl space?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A common starting point, reflected in recent editions of the residential building code, is about 70 pints per day at AHAM conditions for every 1,000 square feet of sealed crawl space. For a typical 800 to 1,300 square foot sealed crawl space, a 55 to 70 pint unit is right. Size up for humid climates, high water tables or incomplete encapsulation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a regular portable dehumidifier in a crawl space?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but it rarely lasts. Portables lose most of their capacity in the cool temperatures common in crawl spaces, spend long periods defrosting, and are not built for years of unattended continuous running. Their plastic housings and short warranties reflect that. A purpose-built crawl space unit costs more upfront but usually costs less over its life.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to encapsulate my crawl space before adding a dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A dehumidifier in a vented crawl space with bare soil is drying outdoor air and ground moisture that never stop arriving. At minimum, cover the floor with a sealed vapor barrier that runs up the walls and close the foundation vents. Without that, even a large unit will run constantly and struggle to hold humidity.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should a crawl space be kept at?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most contractors target 50% to 55% relative humidity. That keeps conditions below the roughly 60% level where mold and dust mites thrive and keeps wood framing dry enough to prevent rot. Going below about 45% wastes energy. Verify the level at the far end of the space with a separate hygrometer rather than trusting the unit's display.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should the dehumidifier water drain in a crawl space?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best destination is a floor drain or sump pit reached by a steadily sloping gravity line. Without a downhill route, use a unit with a built-in pump or add a condensate pump, and discharge outside well away from the foundation. Protect the outside end from freezing and inspect it every season so a blockage does not shut the unit down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run a crawl space dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 55 to 70 pint crawl space unit draws roughly 500 to 600 watts while running. In a well-sealed crawl space it may run a third to half the time in summer and much less in winter, which typically works out to tens of dollars per month in peak season. Efficient models and good encapsulation both cut run time significantly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is an LGR dehumidifier and do I need one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                LGR stands for low grain refrigerant. These units are designed to keep removing moisture as air gets drier and cooler, and they come from the water damage restoration industry. You need one for drying a crawl space after a leak or flood. For a sealed crawl space in steady state, a compact crawl space unit is usually enough and costs less.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

